window.docContents[90].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-edge-colourings":{"contents":"So far we have coloured vertices. A proper edge colouring of G colours the edges\nof G so that edges sharing an endpoint receive different colours. Mathlib has no\nseparate notion of this (its EdgeLabeling assigns labels to edges with no\nproperness condition), and we don't need one. Two edges share an endpoint exactly\nwhen they are adjacent vertices of the line graph L[G] (lineGraph). So a proper\nedge colouring of G with colours γ is just a colouring L[G].Coloring γ. The\nchromatic index of G is the least number of colours in a proper edge colouring,\nso it is χ L[G]. Mathlib does not have it yet, so we define it as an abbrev,\nwhich Lean unfolds automatically when it needs to, and introduce the standard\nnotation χ' G.\n\nThe chromatic index of G: the least number of colours in a proper edge\ncolouring of G, that is, the chromatic number χ L[G] of its line graph./-- The *chromatic index* of G: the least number of colours in a proper edge\ncolouring of G, that is, the chromatic number χ L[G] of its line graph. -/\nnoncomputable abbrev chromaticIndex (G : SimpleGraph V) : ℕ∞ := χ L[G]\n\n\n@[inherit_doc]\nscoped notation \"χ'\" => SimpleGraph.chromaticIndex\n\n\nThe edges at a vertex v pairwise share the endpoint v, so they form a clique in\nL[G]. Hence the chromatic index is at least the degree of any vertex.\n\ntheorem degree_le_chromaticIndex (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] :\n    G.degree v ≤ χ' G := by\n  -- the edges s(v, w) at v, as vertices of L[G]\n  let f : G.neighborSet v ↪ G.edgeSet :=\n    ⟨fun w => ⟨s(v, w), w.2⟩, fun _ _ h => Subtype.ext (Sym2.congr_right.mp\n      (congrArg Subtype.val h))⟩\n  have hc : L[G].IsClique (univ.map f : Finset G.edgeSet) := by\n    simp only [coe_map, coe_univ, Set.image_univ]\n    rintro _ ⟨w, rfl⟩ _ ⟨w', rfl⟩ hne\n    exact ⟨hne, v, Sym2.mem_mk_left _ _, Sym2.mem_mk_left _ _⟩\n  have := hc.card_le_chromaticNumber\n  rwa [card_map, card_univ, card_neighborSet_eq_degree] at this\n\n\nTaking v of maximum degree, the chromatic index of a finite graph is at least its\nmaximum degree Δ(G).\n\ntheorem maxDegree_le_chromaticIndex [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    Δ(G) ≤ χ' G := by\n  cases isEmpty_or_nonempty V\n  · simp\n  · obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := G.exists_maximal_degree_vertex\n    exact hv ▸ G.degree_le_chromaticIndex v\n\nend mycielskian_and_edges\n\n\nVizing's theorem V. G. Vizing, On an estimate of the chromatic class of a p-graph (in Russian), Diskret. Analiz (1964), no. 3, 25–30. says that this lower bound is almost tight:\nχ' G ≤ Δ(G) + 1 for every finite graph G, and Kőnig's line colouring theorem\nD. Kőnig, Über Graphen und ihre Anwendung auf Determinantentheorie und Mengenlehre, Math. Ann. 77 (1916), no. 4, 453–465. says that χ' G = Δ(G) for every finite bipartite graph G.\nNeither is in Mathlib yet. The greedy bound of the next section, applied to L[G],\nwhose vertices have degree at most 2 * Δ(G) - 2, gives the much weaker\nχ' G ≤ 2 * Δ(G) - 1. (Mathlib is also missing many other standard topics of\ncolouring theory such as chromatic polynomials and list colourings.)\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings","header":"7.7. Edge Colourings","id":"/Colorings/#section-edge-colourings"},"/Constructions/#section-blow-ups":{"contents":"While not currently in Mathlib we can define the blow-up of a graph G.\nInformally, this is the graph obtained from G by replacing each vertex with a class\nof vertices and defining edges between vertices in different classes according to the\nedges in G. (This is a trivial generalization of the completeMultipartiteGraph\nconstruction, which is the special case where G is a complete graph.)\n\nThe blow-up of a graph G given by replacing each vertex i with a class of\nvertices V i./-- The blow-up of a graph G given by replacing each vertex i with a class of\nvertices V i. -/\nabbrev blowUpGraph {ι : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph ι) (V : ι → Type*) :\n    SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) := SimpleGraph.comap Sigma.fst G\n\n\nThe n-uniform blow-up of a graph G is the blow-up of G where each vertex is\nreplaced by a class of size n.\n\nThe n-uniform blow-up of a graph G given by replacing each vertex i with\nn vertices (Fin n), denoted G[n]./-- The n-uniform blow-up of a graph G given by replacing each vertex i with\nn vertices (Fin n), denoted G[n]. -/\nabbrev blowUpN {ι : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph ι) (n : ℕ) :\n    SimpleGraph (Σ _ : ι, Fin n) := G.blowUpGraph fun _ => Fin n\n\n\nWe introduce the notation G[n] for the n-uniform blow-up of G.\n\nG[n] is the n-uniform blow-up of G, which replaces each vertex of G with\nn vertices (Fin n)./-- G[n] is the n-uniform blow-up of G, which replaces each vertex of G with\nn vertices (Fin n). -/\nscoped notation:max G \"[\" n \"]\" => blowUpN G n\n\n\n#check C[4][3] -- the blow-up of C[4] replacing each vertex by a class of size 3\nexample : C[4][3].Adj ⟨1, 0⟩ ⟨2, 0⟩ := by decide\nexample : ¬C[4][3].Adj ⟨0, 0⟩ ⟨2, 1⟩ := by decide\n\n\nC[4][3]: the 3-uniform blow-up of C[4]\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Complete Multipartite Graphs and Blow-ups","header":"3.6.2. Blow-ups","id":"/Constructions/#section-blow-ups"},"/Constructions/#section-cayley-graphs":{"contents":"Cayley graphs A. Cayley, Desiderata and suggestions: No. 2. The theory of groups: graphical representation, Amer. J. Math. 1 (1878), no. 2, 174–176. can also be found in Mathlib. They are defined for\nany vertex type with a multiplication or addition operation, not just for groups.\n\nIf you look at the definition you will see the use of the @[to_additive] attribute\nto give both multiplicative and additive versions of the same construction. (In\nparticular if you look for the source code for addCayley, you will be taken to\nmulCayley.)\n\n#source mulCayley\n\n\nThe symmetric group on four points is Equiv.Perm (Fin 4) in Mathlib, and its\nelements can be written in cycle notation using c[...]. Taking a 3-cycle and a\n4-cycle as generators gives the graph drawn below.\n\nThe Cayley graph of the symmetric group on Fin 4 generated by a 3-cycle and a\n4-cycle./-- The Cayley graph of the symmetric group on Fin 4 generated by a 3-cycle and a\n4-cycle. -/\nabbrev cayleyS4 : SimpleGraph (Equiv.Perm (Fin 4)) :=\n  mulCayley {c[0, 1, 2], c[0, 1, 2, 3]}\n\n\nIt has one vertex for each of the 24 permutations and is 4-regular, since each\ngenerator accounts for two of the edges at every vertex: one to the neighbour it\nmultiplies to, and one back from its inverse.\n\n#eval Fintype.card (Equiv.Perm (Fin 4)) -- 24\n#eval cayleyS4.degree 1 -- 4\n#eval cayleyS4.edgeFinset.card -- 48\n\n\nThe two edge colours in the figure are the two generators: the triangles are the\nedges contributed by the 3-cycle and the squares those contributed by the\n4-cycle. Splitting the graph up like this is exactly mulCayley_union, which says\nthat a union of generating sets gives the union of the corresponding Cayley graphs.\n\nexample : cayleyS4 = mulCayley {c[0, 1, 2]} ⊔ mulCayley {c[0, 1, 2, 3]} := by\n  rw [← mulCayley_union, Set.singleton_union]\n\n\nThe Cayley graph cayleyS4 of Equiv.Perm (Fin 4), generated by a 3-cycle and a 4-cycle\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Algebraic Constructions","header":"3.2.2. Cayley Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-cayley-graphs"},"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-density-and-the-erd-s-stone-theorem":{"contents":"How does extremalNumber n H grow with n? Dividing by the number of possible edges\nn.choose 2 gives a sequence that is decreasing and so converges. Its limit is the\nTurán density (turanDensity).\n\nStatements about limits in Mathlib use filters:\nTendsto f atTop (𝓝 a) says that f n → a as n → ∞.\n\nsection asymptotics\nopen Filter Asymptotics\n\n#source turanDensity\n#check tendsto_turanDensity\n\n\nWhen the Turán density is nonzero, extremalNumber n H is asymptotically\nturanDensity H * n.choose 2, which Mathlib writes with ~[atTop]\n(asymptotic equivalence).\n\n#check isEquivalent_extremalNumber -- ex (n, H) ~ H.turanDensity * n.choose 2\n\n\nPhrases like \"for all sufficiently large n\" are written with ∀ᶠ (n : ℕ) in atTop.\nFor example, for any ε > 0, every large enough graph with at least\n(H.turanDensity + ε) * n.choose 2 edges contains a copy of H.\n\n#check eventually_isContained_of_card_edgeFinset\n\n\nThe Erdős–Stone theorem P. Erdős and A. H. Stone, On the structure of linear graphs, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 52 (1946), no. 12, 1087–1091. says that a large graph whose\ndensity is a little above 1 - 1 / r, the Turán density of K[r + 1] (by Turán's\ntheorem), contains not just K[r + 1] but a complete (r + 1)-partite graph with\nlarge parts: completeEquipartiteGraph (r + 1) t.\n\nMathlib has the minimum degree version of this theorem.\n\n#check eventually_completeEquipartiteGraph_isContained_of_minDegree\n\nend asymptotics\n\n\nThe Erdős–Stone–Simonovits formula P. Erdős and M. Simonovits, A limit theorem in graph theory, Studia Sci. Math. Hungar. 1 (1966), 51–57., which follows from\nthe Erdős–Stone theorem, is not yet in Mathlib: if χ H = r + 2 then\nturanDensity H = 1 - 1 / (r + 1). (Writing r + 2 excludes an H with no edges:\nit is contained in every large graph, so its Turán density is 0, while\n1 - 1 / 0 = 1 in Lean.)\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Turán's Theorem, Stability and Asymptotics","header":"13.2.7. Turán Density and the Erdős–Stone Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-density-and-the-erd-s-stone-theorem"}});